# 5. Cost logic and investment priorities

The investment should be staged so that early spending buys information and time, while larger spending is released only when independence is technically credible.

| Initiative | Early cost profile | Main benefit | Recommended posture |
|---|---|---|---|
| Strategic inventory | Immediate working capital and carrying cost | Buys time against disruption | Do now |
| Contract protections | Legal and negotiation cost; may require concessions | Improves visibility, price, capacity, and transfer rights | Do now, but do not rely on it alone |
| Alternate-source qualification | Engineering, tooling, testing, and duplicate quality work | Creates physical supply independence | Start now |
| Tooling/process control | Capital and technical-development cost | Removes the primary transfer bottleneck | Pursue in parallel |
| Partial integration | Moderate to high fixed cost; lower utilization initially | Protects critical process and emergency output | Evaluate after process mapping |
| Product redesign | High engineering and launch cost; schedule risk | Strongest long-term supplier independence | Develop as parallel option |

A useful internal investment model would compare each option on:

- Avoided expected disruption loss;
- Reduction in price escalation exposure;
- Reduction in competitor-control risk;
- Working-capital impact;
- Incremental unit cost;
- Engineering and qualification cost;
- Time to effectiveness;
- Reversibility;
- Effect on the next product generation.

The company should not evaluate the second source only on quoted piece price. The relevant metric is **risk-adjusted landed cost**, including qualification, quality, logistics, working capital, and the value of continuity.

---

# 6. Escalation triggers

The company should define triggers before the next crisis, so that escalation is not delayed by optimism or negotiation fatigue.

## Trigger level 1: enhanced monitoring

Activate when any of the following occurs:

- On-time delivery falls below the contractual target for two consecutive months;
- Lead time increases by more than 15%;
- A second price increase occurs within 12 months;
- Supplier capacity or staffing becomes unclear;
- The supplier delays tooling maintenance or technical responses;
- The parent company begins consolidating operations or changing account personnel;
- Forecasts are not acknowledged or capacity is not confirmed.

**Actions:**

- Increase inventory coverage;
- Move to weekly executive reviews;
- Freeze nonessential supplier-dependent product changes;
- Accelerate alternate-source testing;
- Require a written recovery plan.

## Trigger level 2: formal mitigation escalation

Activate when:

- On-time delivery falls below approximately 90% for two consecutive months;
- A delivery is more than 30 days late;
- The supplier misses a confirmed capacity allocation;
- Price increases exceed the agreed formula or cumulative annual threshold;
- Quality rejects or corrective-action events exceed agreed limits;
- The supplier refuses reasonable tooling, data, or audit rights;
- Confidentiality controls cannot be verified;
- The competitor parent receives access to commercially sensitive information;
- Inventory falls below the approved strategic reserve.

**Actions:**

- Raise inventory to the approved upper limit;
- Shift available volume to the alternate source or development partner;
- Authorize tooling replication or internal pilot capability;
- Escalate contract remedies;
- Begin redesign prototype work at accelerated pace;
- Consider executive or board-level engagement with the supplier’s parent.

## Trigger level 3: independence program

Activate when:

- The supplier threatens termination, allocation reduction, or supply interruption;
- A change-of-control event creates an unacceptable competitive conflict;
- The supplier is unable or unwilling to confirm capacity for the next 6–12 months;
- Delivery performance falls below 85% or production stoppage occurs;
- A material quality or safety incident occurs;
- The supplier imposes an economically unacceptable price increase;
- Tooling is inaccessible, damaged, or not maintained;
- The supplier refuses to support transfer or breaches confidentiality;
- The expected disruption cost exceeds the remaining cost of redesign or internalization.

**Actions:**

- Release strategic inventory under crisis controls;
- Move all qualified volume to the alternate source;
- Activate internal or contract pilot production;
- Stop new product development that increases dependence;
- Accelerate product redesign and customer/regulatory approval;
- Seek acquisition of a process specialist or critical supplier capability if necessary;
- Prepare legal remedies, emergency tooling recovery, and customer communication.

## Trigger level 4: exit from the supplier

Exit should be considered when:

- An alternate source has demonstrated stable quality and delivery over a defined period;
- Internal or partner capacity can cover the remaining demand;
- The current supplier’s commercial or competitive conflict is no longer acceptable;
- The supplier fails a major contractual or quality obligation;
- The company can transition without exceeding customer-service and inventory limits.

A full exit need not mean abandoning the supplier permanently. The company may retain the supplier as a qualified backup if the relationship remains commercially and strategically acceptable.

