A cross-functional critical-component team should report monthly to executive management and quarterly to the board. It should include:

- Procurement;
- Engineering;
- Quality;
- Operations;
- Finance;
- Legal;
- Information security;
- Product management;
- Customer service.

The team should own:

- Inventory position;
- Qualification milestones;
- Tooling and technical-data rights;
- Supplier performance;
- Redesign progress;
- Cost-to-independence;
- Trigger monitoring;
- Exit readiness.

The dashboard should include:

### Supply

- On-time delivery;
- Lead-time variability;
- Confirmed capacity;
- Open-order aging;
- Quality rejection rate;
- Corrective-action status.

### Resilience

- Months of available inventory;
- Percentage of demand technically qualified at the alternate source;
- Percentage of demand commercially supplied by the alternate source;
- Tooling duplication status;
- Process-documentation completeness;
- Internal or partner emergency capacity.

### Economics

- Current and forecast component price;
- Price variance against contract;
- Carrying cost of inventory;
- Qualification spend;
- Incremental dual-source cost;
- Estimated cost of redesign;
- Expected loss from a disruption scenario.

### Strategic exposure

- Sensitive information shared with the supplier;
- Ownership and control changes;
- Dependency on supplier personnel;
- Degree of competitor access;
- Exit readiness.

One executive should be accountable for the overall independence plan. The current supplier relationship owner should not be the only person deciding whether the company progresses toward an alternate source; commercial incentives can otherwise quietly favor continued dependency.

